ADS-011 — Relationship Model

Document ID: ADS-011
Title: Relationship Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Relationship Model (CRM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Relationship Model establishes standardized, typed, directed, and governed relationships between Canonical Atlas Objects (CAOs), enabling semantic navigation, traceability, dependency analysis, reasoning, and interoperability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Relationship model
Relationship types
Relationship semantics
Cardinality
Directionality
Constraints
Lineage
Dependency model
Governance

This specification applies to all relationships between Canonical Atlas Objects.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Relationship is a governed, typed, and directional association between two or more Canonical Atlas Objects that expresses a semantic connection without modifying the participating objects.

Relationships describe how objects are connected.

They do not alter the meaning of the connected objects.

4. Objectives

The Relationship Model SHALL:

Standardize object relationships.
Preserve semantic meaning.
Enable traceability.
Support reasoning.
Support dependency analysis.
Support knowledge graph construction.
Support interoperability.
Enable impact analysis.
5. Relationship Principles

Every Canonical Relationship SHALL be:

Explicit
Typed
Directional
Traceable
Governed
Version-aware
Independently identifiable
Immutable after publication
6. Canonical Relationship Structure

Every relationship SHALL include:

Property	Description
Relationship ID	Globally unique identifier
Relationship Type	Canonical semantic type
Source Object	Originating CAO
Target Object	Destination CAO
Direction	Relationship direction
Status	Operational state
Created Timestamp	Creation time
Version	Relationship version

Relationships SHALL themselves be governed entities.

7. Canonical Relationship Types

Atlas defines the following canonical relationship categories.

Structural Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
contains	Parent contains child
partOf	Child belongs to parent
composedOf	Composite structure
decomposesInto	Structural decomposition
Dependency Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
dependsOn	Requires another object
blocks	Prevents progression
enables	Allows execution
influences	Affects outcome
Provenance Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
derivesFrom	Originates from
generatedBy	Created by
transformedFrom	Modified from
validatedBy	Verified through
Cognitive Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
supports	Provides supporting evidence
contradicts	Conflicts with
explains	Provides explanation
concludes	Produces conclusion
Decision Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
approvedBy	Approved through
rejectedBy	Rejected through
supersedes	Replaces
implements	Realizes decision
Operational Relationships
Relationship	Meaning
triggers	Initiates processing
consumes	Uses object
produces	Generates object
communicatesWith	Exchanges information
8. Cardinality

Atlas supports:

Cardinality	Description
1 → 1	One-to-one
1 → N	One-to-many
N → 1	Many-to-one
N → N	Many-to-many

Cardinality SHALL be explicitly declared.

9. Directionality

Relationships SHALL be directional.

Example:

Evidence
    │
supports
    ▼
Conclusion

The reverse direction SHALL NOT be inferred unless explicitly defined.

10. Relationship Constraints

Relationships MAY define constraints including:

Domain restrictions
Object type compatibility
Multiplicity
Lifecycle compatibility
Security compatibility
Policy compatibility

Constraint violations SHALL invalidate the relationship.

11. Lineage Model

Every Canonical Atlas Object SHALL support lineage traversal.

Example:

Observation
      │
      ▼
Evidence
      │
      ▼
Understanding
      │
      ▼
Knowledge
      │
      ▼
Decision
      │
      ▼
Implementation

Lineage SHALL remain reconstructable throughout the object lifecycle.

12. Dependency Model

Dependencies SHALL distinguish between:

Dependency Type	Description
Mandatory	Required for operation
Optional	Enhances operation
Conditional	Required under specified conditions
Temporal	Depends on sequencing

Dependencies SHALL be explicitly classified.

13. Relationship Lifecycle

Every Canonical Relationship SHALL support:

Defined
   │
Validated
   │
Active
   │
Superseded
   │
Archived

Relationships SHALL NOT be deleted.

Historical relationships SHALL remain traceable.

14. Governance

Every Canonical Relationship SHALL define:

Owner
Steward
Validation authority
Security classification
Retention policy
Audit requirements

Relationship governance SHALL remain independent of participating objects.

15. Quality Requirements

Every relationship SHALL satisfy:

Semantic correctness
Consistency
Traceability
Integrity
Validity
Completeness
Interoperability
Explainability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

16. Conformance

A Canonical Relationship conforms if it:

Uses canonical relationship types.
References valid Canonical Atlas Objects.
Defines direction.
Declares cardinality.
Preserves governance.
Supports lifecycle management.
Maintains traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Semantic inference rules.
Ontology-aligned relationships.
Cross-enterprise relationship federation.
Temporal relationships.
Probabilistic relationships.
Digital Twin relationship profiles.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical relationship semantics.

